From 2ce36c0af1eaff5496678f80a18bdaff9414361d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: taekim <taekim@day34.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 17:39:36 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 38/50] Answer the diagonal question once, where the parser
 already knew it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A cell diagonal is stated in three places — the line TYPE in DiagonalLine,
the DIRECTION in two nibbles of BorderFill.attr, and a bit that says the
whole thing is a centre line instead. A host given only the raw fields has
to combine them correctly, and getting it wrong is not subtle: two thirds
of the declarations in a 637-document corpus carry a type but no direction,
so a host that judged by type alone would rule lines across cells the
document left plain.

The parser already had the rule, in TableOps' own editing path. Moving it
to BorderFill::cell_diagonal makes it one answer rather than two that can
drift, and exporting it as `cellDiagonal` hands hosts the answer instead of
the puzzle — the same reasoning this file already records for `bg`.

Measured, following cell references rather than fill declarations: 47 of
637 documents draw one, holding 208 cells. All three directions occur.
